WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.391
Tooling makes secure C++ more repeatable.

2
00:00:02.391 --> 00:00:09.563
Compiler warnings can identify suspicious conversions, deprecated APIs, unreachable logic, missing virtual destructors, risky control flow, and other

3
00:00:09.563 --> 00:00:10.759
patterns worth review.

4
00:00:10.759 --> 00:00:17.931
A warning is not the same as a defect, but ignored warnings become noise that hides real problems.

5
00:00:17.931 --> 00:00:23.111
Sanitizers can expose memory errors, undefined behavior, thread races, and leaks during testing.

6
00:00:23.111 --> 00:00:28.690
They are especially useful for code that handles buffers, parsers, object lifetimes, and concurrency.

7
00:00:28.690 --> 00:00:35.066
Sanitizer findings should lead to understanding the underlying contract, not just patching the immediate stack trace.

8
00:00:35.066 --> 00:00:43.035
Static analysis can help identify unchecked input, ownership confusion, unsafe APIs, inconsistent assumptions, and error paths that leave objects in

9
00:00:43.035 --> 00:00:43.832
questionable states.

10
00:00:43.832 --> 00:00:50.606
It does not replace code review, but it gives reviewers a map of areas that deserve attention.

11
00:00:50.606 --> 00:00:58.177
Tests should include boundary inputs, malformed data, failure paths, large inputs, empty inputs, concurrency edge cases, and dependency error

12
00:00:58.177 --> 00:00:58.576
behavior.

13
00:00:58.576 --> 00:01:01.763
Secure tests are not only happy-path examples.

14
00:01:01.763 --> 00:01:09.334
They show that the program rejects invalid data, cleans up resources, and does not continue after an unsafe state.

15
00:01:09.334 --> 00:01:16.905
Fuzzing can support robustness testing at a high level when parsers, protocols, file formats, or serialized data are present.

16
00:01:16.905 --> 00:01:25.672
The goal is to find unexpected crashes, hangs, assertion failures, and invalid states during controlled testing so teams can harden validation and

17
00:01:25.672 --> 00:01:27.266
error handling before release.

18
00:01:27.266 --> 00:01:34.438
Secure code review should trace trust boundaries, ownership, lifetime, validation, error handling, dependency use, concurrency, and logging behavior.

19
00:01:34.438 --> 00:01:43.205
Reviewers should ask where data comes from, who owns each object, what happens on failure, which assumptions are enforced, and whether diagnostics

20
00:01:43.205 --> 00:01:44.400
reveal too much.
